```{r helpers, include = FALSE}
source(file.path(getOption("bootmakr.precompute.dir"), "helpers.R"))
```

A regression coefficient from observational data is only as credible as the
assumption that no important variable was left out. `sensemakr` (Cinelli &
Hazlett, 2020) makes that assumption concrete: it reports the coefficient you
would have obtained had a variable as strong as one of your observed
covariates been omitted. `bootmakr` adds a standard error, a confidence
interval and a *p*-value for that adjusted coefficient, obtained by
resampling the data the way you would choose standard errors for the
regression itself. This is needed because the usual tools, the impact
threshold of a confounding variable (ITCV) and the analytic interval of
`sensemakr`, break down when the regression calls for robust standard errors
(Lonati & Wulff, 2026); [Why bootstrap?](https://jespernwulff.github.io/bootmakr/articles/why-bootstrap.html)
shows why. This page shows a first analysis from start to finish. The same
steps in Stata are in
[Get started in Stata](https://jespernwulff.github.io/bootmakr/articles/stata.html).

## Installation

`bootmakr` is installed from GitHub. `sensemakr`, which it builds on, comes
from CRAN and is installed along with it.

```{r install, eval = FALSE}
# install.packages("remotes")
remotes::install_github("jespernwulff/bootmakr")
```

## The example data

The package comes with `firms`, a simulated panel of 250 firms observed for
20 years. Because the data are simulated, we know the truth: the effect of
`x` on `y` is 0.25. Two firm-level characteristics affect both `x` and `y`,
an observed control `c` and a variable `q` that we pretend not to have. The
within-firm components of `x` and of the disturbance are persistent, so
standard errors must be clustered by firm. (The data are a variant of the
simulated example in Lonati and Wulff, 2026; see `?firms` for how they were
generated.)

```{r data}
library(bootmakr)
data("firms")
head(firms)
```

Here is the regression a researcher without `q` would run.

```{r naive}
coef(lm(y ~ x + c, data = firms))["x"]
```

```{r naive-numbers, include = FALSE}
b0 <- coef(lm(y ~ x + c, data = firms))[["x"]]
```

The coefficient is `r num(b0)` instead of 0.25, because `q` drives both `x`
and `y`. With real data you would not know this. What you can ask is how the
result would change if a variable as strong as something you *do* observe
had been left out. That is the question `bootmakr` answers.

## A first analysis

`sensemakr` needs a *benchmark*: an observed covariate that sets the scale
for the omitted variable. We take `c` and ask what the coefficient of `x`
would be had a variable *as strong as* `c` been omitted. In these data that
is exactly what `q` is.

```{r first, cache = TRUE}
first <- bootmakr(y ~ x + c, data = firms, treat = "x",
                  benchmark_covariates = "c",
                  cluster = "firm",
                  seed = 123, progress = FALSE)
first
```

```{r first-numbers, include = FALSE}
r1 <- first$results
ob <- first$benchmark_strength$observed
im <- first$benchmark_strength$implied
```

The call names the model, the treatment (`treat`), the benchmark
(`benchmark_covariates`) and, because observations of the same firm are not
independent, the variable that identifies the clusters to resample
(`cluster`). Everything else is left at its default: an omitted variable
exactly as strong as the benchmark (`kd = 1`) and 1,000 bootstrap
replications. `seed` makes the result reproducible, and `progress = FALSE`
switches off the progress bar, which is useful on screen but not in a
document. The run takes about 15 seconds.

### Reading the output

**The adjusted estimate.** Had a variable as strong as `c` been omitted, the
coefficient of `x` would be `r num(r1$estimate, 3)` rather than
`r num(b0, 3)`. This is the estimate `sensemakr` reports for the same
benchmark, and it is close to the true 0.25. It does not involve the
bootstrap.

**Its uncertainty.** The remaining columns come from the bootstrap. In each
of the 1,000 replications `bootmakr` draws firms with replacement, refits the
regression and recomputes the adjusted estimate. The standard deviation of
those 1,000 values is the standard error (`r num(r1$se, 3)`), their 2.5th and
97.5th percentiles form the 95 percent confidence interval
(`r num(r1$ci_lower, 3)` to `r num(r1$ci_upper, 3)`), and the *p*-value
(`r num(r1$pvalue, 3, lead = FALSE)`) is twice the smaller of the two shares
of replications that fall on either side of zero. The effect of `x` remains
positive and statistically distinguishable from zero even if an omitted
variable as strong as `c` is at work.

**The benchmark strength.** The last block says what "as strong as `c`"
means. `c` explains `r num(100 * ob$r2dxj.x, 1)` percent of the variance of
`x` and `r num(100 * ob$r2yxj.dx, 1)` percent of the variance of `y` that
`x` leaves unexplained. The omitted variable this implies has an *impact* of
`r num(im$impact, 2, lead = FALSE)`: the product of its partial correlations
with `y` and with `x`, which is the scale on which the ITCV is stated. All of
this is computed from the data. No standard error is involved, so the block
is the same whatever variance estimator you prefer. See
[How strong is the benchmark?](https://jespernwulff.github.io/bootmakr/articles/benchmark-strength.html)
for the details.

## From one strength to many: the breakdown point

An omitted variable as strong as `c` does not overturn the result. How much
stronger would it have to be? Give `kd` several values and `bootmakr`
evaluates each of them on the same bootstrap samples.

```{r sweep, cache = TRUE}
sweep <- bootmakr(y ~ x + c, data = firms, treat = "x",
                  benchmark_covariates = "c",
                  kd = seq(0.5, 1.5, by = 0.25),
                  cluster = "firm",
                  seed = 123, progress = FALSE)
sweep
```

```{r sweep-plot, fig.cap = "Adjusted estimate and bootstrap 95 percent confidence interval for an omitted variable 0.5 to 1.5 times as strong as c. Solid markers: p < .05."}
plot(sweep, type = "kd_sweep")
```

```{r sweep-numbers, include = FALSE}
rs <- sweep$results
last_sig  <- max(rs$kd[rs$pvalue < 0.05])
first_not <- min(rs$kd[rs$pvalue >= 0.05])
```

The adjusted estimate shrinks as the assumed omitted variable grows stronger,
and its confidence interval widens. In this run the result holds up to an
omitted variable `r last_sig` times as strong as `c` and breaks down at
`r first_not` times: from there on the interval includes zero. That
*breakdown point* is a compact summary of how robust the finding is. (A
*p*-value printed as 0 means that none of the 1,000 replications fell on the
other side of zero.)

## Checking the answer

Simulated data have one advantage over real ones: the omitted variable is in
the file. The regression that includes `q` gives

```{r check}
coef(lm(y ~ x + c + q, data = firms))["x"]
```

```{r check-numbers, include = FALSE}
bq <- coef(lm(y ~ x + c + q, data = firms))[["x"]]
covered <- r1$ci_lower < bq && bq < r1$ci_upper
if (!covered) stop("The interval at kd = 1 no longer covers the with-q coefficient: revise the text.")
```

which lies inside the bootstrap interval at `kd = 1`. The
[Why bootstrap?](https://jespernwulff.github.io/bootmakr/articles/why-bootstrap.html)
article uses the same data to show what the ITCV and the analytic interval
of `sensemakr` make of this example.

## Working with the results

The object returned by `bootmakr()` keeps everything that was computed.

```{r results}
sweep$results
```

`estimate` to `pvalue` are the columns of the printed table. `r2dz.x` and
`r2yz.dx` are the partial R^2^ values of the omitted variable assumed in each
row, and `impact` its impact. The benchmark-strength block is stored as a
list.

```{r strength}
first$benchmark_strength
```

The bootstrap draws themselves are in `$boot_samples`, one column per value
of `kd`, so the distribution can be inspected without rerunning anything.

```{r histogram, fig.height = 4, fig.cap = "Bootstrap distribution of the adjusted estimate at kd = 1. The dashed line marks the estimate in the original sample."}
quantile(first$boot_samples[, 1], c(0.025, 0.5, 0.975))

plot(first, type = "histogram")
```

Finally, `$sensemakr_orig` holds the `sensemakr` object of the original fit,
so everything `sensemakr` offers (its summary, robustness values and contour
plots) remains one step away.

## The choices you make

**The benchmark.** Choose an observed covariate that is strong and that you
can argue an omitted variable is unlikely to exceed. Report why you chose it.
A group of covariates can serve as a joint benchmark with
`gbenchmark_covariates = c("x1", "x2")`.

**The multiple.** `kd` scales the benchmark's relationship with the
treatment and `ky` its relationship with the outcome. `ky` follows `kd`
unless you set it.

**The resampling scheme.** It should mirror how the data were sampled and how
you would compute standard errors in the regression itself. Without
`cluster`, rows are resampled, which allows for heteroskedasticity. With
`cluster = "id"`, whole clusters are resampled. `cluster = c("id1", "id2")`
requests two-way clustering, and `strata` a stratified bootstrap. See
[Clustered and stratified data](https://jespernwulff.github.io/bootmakr/articles/clustering.html).

**Replications and seed.** The default of 1,000 replications is fine for a
first look. For results you report, use more (5,000 or 10,000), set a `seed`,
and when the verdict is close to the significance threshold check that it
has settled with the `converge` argument.

## Where next

- The [worked example](https://jespernwulff.github.io/bootmakr/articles/ceo-pay.html)
  takes a published study through a complete analysis: the choice of
  standard errors, the benchmark, the breakdown point, a grouped benchmark
  and the convergence check.
- [Why bootstrap?](https://jespernwulff.github.io/bootmakr/articles/why-bootstrap.html)
  shows, on these data, why the ITCV and the analytic interval of
  `sensemakr` cannot be trusted with robust standard errors.
- `?bootmakr` documents every argument, `?plot.bootmakr` the three plots,
  and `?firms` the data.
- The Stata command of the same name runs the same analysis. See
  [R and Stata side by side](https://jespernwulff.github.io/bootmakr/articles/r-and-stata.html).

## References

Cinelli, C., & Hazlett, C. (2020). Making sense of sensitivity: Extending
omitted variable bias. *Journal of the Royal Statistical Society: Series B*,
82(1), 39--67. <https://doi.org/10.1111/rssb.12348>

Cinelli, C., Ferwerda, J., & Hazlett, C. (2024). sensemakr: Sensitivity
analysis tools for OLS in R and Stata. *Observational Studies*, 10(2),
93--127. <https://doi.org/10.1353/obs.2024.a946583>

Lonati, S., & Wulff, J. N. (2026). Why you should not use the ITCV with
robust standard errors (and what to do instead). *Academy of Management
Proceedings*, 2026(1). <https://doi.org/10.5465/AMPROC.2026.247bp>
